        <!-- ===================== §1 · WHAT THE SEARCH RETURNS ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="search">
          <h2>What Does “African Grey Parrot for Sale Near Me” Actually Return?</h2>
          <p class="opener">A near-me search returns whoever has a pin in your town: bird shops, rescues and classified ads, in that order. A breeder who hatched the bird is usually hours away, so a page like this one exists to hand you to the state page that fits, then get out of the way.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "near-me-search-result-anatomy-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "near-me-search-result-anatomy-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "near-me-search-result-anatomy-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Near-me search results dominated by location-specific listings above national pages">
          <p>We measured it on a Maryland search: the map pack held three retail bird shops and no breeder at all, and seven of the nine organic results named a state or a city in their address. That is not a flaw in the search. It is the search doing exactly what you asked, which is why the answer for a buyer is a state page, not a national one.</p>

          <h3>Why Your Search Shows Pet Shops and Classified Ads</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "why-near-me-shows-shops-and-classifieds-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "why-near-me-shows-shops-and-classifieds-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "why-near-me-shows-shops-and-classifieds-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="How a near-me search sorts shops and classifieds above real breeders">
          <p>Shops have a storefront, a phone number and a few hundred reviews, so the map trusts them. Classified sites have a page for every metro in the country, so the organic results trust them. A family aviary has one address, and ours is in a town most buyers will never drive through. We do not compete for the pin. We compete on what happens after you click.</p>

          <h4>Is the Nearest Bird Shop the Same Thing as a Breeder?</h4>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "is-a-bird-shop-the-same-as-a-breeder-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "is-a-bird-shop-the-same-as-a-breeder-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "is-a-bird-shop-the-same-as-a-breeder-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="A retail bird shop set beside a family aviary, side by side">
          <p>No, and the difference is who was awake for the night feeds. A shop buys weaned birds in from wherever it can and sells them on; the person behind the counter may never have seen the parents. We hatched every bird on this page, and the answer to “what does she do at dusk” comes from the people who watched her do it. A good shop is a fine place to buy a cage. It is a second-hand place to buy a bird.</p>

          <h5>A Shop Resells Birds It Did Not Hatch, Hand-Feed, or Wean</h5>
          <p>Ask any retailer three questions: who were the parents, who did the hand-feeding, and what did the bird weigh at weaning. A breeder answers all three from memory. A shop reads you the supplier's invoice, if it has one. The gap between those two answers is the whole reason people search for a breeder in the first place, and it does not close because the shop is closer.</p>

          <h6>Distance Note: Why We Are Not in Your Local Results</h6>
          <p>Because we are in one place and you are in another, and no amount of writing changes the map. What we can change is the page you reach: one written for your state, with the route, the cost and the birds already worked out. That is what the grid below is for.</p>

          <h3>Grey or Gray, Parrot or Parrots — All One Search</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "grey-or-gray-parrot-or-parrots-one-search-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "grey-or-gray-parrot-or-parrots-one-search-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "grey-or-gray-parrot-or-parrots-one-search-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Four spellings buyers type that all reach the same aviary">
          <p>Buyers type African gray parrot for sale near me about as often as they type grey, and more of the gray spellers click through, so we use both on purpose. Parrot or parrots, African Grey breeders near me or African grey for sale near me, where to buy or who sells: every one of those phrasings is the same person asking the same thing. One bird, two spellings, and a page that answers all of them rather than pretending the spelling picks a species. The bird has never corrected anyone's spelling. It corrects most other things.</p>
