          <h3>Our Congos, and Where Each Could Fly</h3>
          <p>The Congos, <em>Psittacus erithacus</em>, are the larger birds with the scarlet tail, and we have {groupCount("congo") + groupCount("pair")} on the page counting the pair. Bery has done her growing and takes a long flight day well. Amie is still on formula, so her state is whichever one you are in by the time she eats on her own. Roys would fly anywhere and inspect the crate on the way; a city with a direct flight from here suits him best because he is the one who will be awake for all of it.</p>

          <h3>Our Timnehs, and Where Each Could Fly</h3>
          <p>Elad and Evie are <em>Psittacus timneh</em>, the smaller species, darker in the body with a maroon tail, and both are weaned and priced in the {timnehRange} band. They weigh {timnehWeight} grams against a Congo's {congoWeight}, so they ride in the smaller crate at the same {airport}. Evie prefers a quiet handover, so an early airport collection beats a doorstep with the whole family waiting; Elad is easier about it and steps onto a hand the moment the crate door opens.</p>

          <h3>The Companion Pair</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "can-i-reserve-a-bird-before-i-travel-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "can-i-reserve-a-bird-before-i-travel-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "can-i-reserve-a-bird-before-i-travel-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Holding a named bird with a deposit before travelling to meet it">
          <p>Jins and Jeni are two unrelated hand-raised Congos we place together as a companion pair, {ceiling} for both, one crate, one destination. For a pair we point buyers at home delivery rather than the cargo desk, because two birds and a double crate in the back of a car at the end of a long day is one thing more than most people planned for. They are the birds we would least like to see in a hurried handover.</p>
          <p>Whichever bird it is, it was raised under what we call <em>The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol</em>: hand-fed in our kitchen on a schedule we keep ourselves, and held here until it is weaned and eating unaided between twelve and sixteen weeks. Its manners around people come from <em>The Midland Socialization Method</em>, which is our name for the family handling and daily out-of-cage routine every chick gets before it is ever listed. Both are our names for our own practice, not certificates anyone issued us.</p>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="fact" source="https://www.iucnredlist.org/species/22724813/154068029" />An African Grey lives 40 to 60 years in a home, so a bird matched to your household this year may still be in it in the 2070s. That is the reason we would rather you took a week over the choice than an afternoon.</p>

          <h4>Can I Reserve a Bird Before I Travel to See It?</h4>
          <p>Yes, after a video call. A {deposit} deposit holds a named bird while you sort out a flight date or a weekend to drive down, and it comes off the balance. We will not take one from somebody who has only seen a photograph, because a photograph is the one thing every fake listing in the country also has.</p>

          <h5>A Deposit Holds a Named Bird — It Never Buys a Photograph</h5>
          <p>The distinction matters more for a buyer at a distance than for one who can drive here. When you cannot walk into the room, the live call is the room: you watch the bird step up, you ask it something it has to answer today, and you see the band on its leg. Then the deposit means something. Before that, it only means you trust a picture.</p>

          <h6>Ask Before You Drive: What to Confirm First</h6>
          <aside class="k4" aria-label="Ask before you drive">
            <p class="k4-h">Four things to confirm before you get in the car or book a flight</p>
            <ul class="k4-list">
              <li><span set:html={tick} /><span>That the bird is weaned and eating on its own, with a date, not “nearly”.</span></li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /><span>That the sexing certificate and the vet certificate already exist in the bird's name.</span></li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /><span>Which cargo counter, or which door, the bird is booked to, and on what day.</span></li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /><span>Who answers the phone on arrival day if something looks wrong.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </aside>
          <p>Every one of those has a plain answer from us, and the answers are the same whether you are collecting in person or meeting a crate. A seller who cannot give them is not ready to place a bird, however near they are.</p>
