        <!-- ===================== §5 · CHEAP AND THE CLASSIFIEDS ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="cheap">
          <h2>Why Are “Cheap” African Greys Near You Usually a Scam?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Because the cheapest thing in this trade to produce is a listing, and the most expensive is a weaned bird. The word cheap sits on every near-me search suggestion we measured, and a classified site sits fourth in the results. Those two facts are related.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "same-african-grey-three-classified-prices-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "same-african-grey-three-classified-prices-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "same-african-grey-three-classified-prices-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The same African Grey advertised at three different prices on three classified sites">
          <p>On one Maryland results page we saw the species offered at $800 and at $5,555 within a few lines of each other. Neither number described a bird we could examine. Our floor is {floor} because hand-feeding, the pre-sale avian examination, the screening panel, the sexing assay and the paperwork cost what they cost, and a listing that undercuts that has left something out. <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Our full guide to fake listings</a> goes through the payment routes and pressure lines; this section covers only what the near-me searcher runs into.</p>

          <h3>What Classified Listings Leave Out</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-classified-listings-leave-out-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-classified-listings-leave-out-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-classified-listings-leave-out-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The parts a classified advertisement tears away before you arrive">
          <p>The parents, the weaning weight, the veterinarian's name, the band number and an address. A classified ad has a photo, a price and a phone number, and the photo often belongs to someone else's bird; run it through a reverse image search and see how many other listings it appears on. That is not always fraud; sometimes it is a shop reselling without records. The result for you is the same: nothing to check.</p>

          <h4>What Did We Measure Across the Listing Sites?</h4>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-we-measured-across-the-listing-sites-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-we-measured-across-the-listing-sites-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-we-measured-across-the-listing-sites-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="One seller's listings pinned together across three separate sites">
          <p>We followed one seller's birds across three sites on a single August afternoon and wrote down the asking price on each. The photographs matched. The prices did not, by thousands of dollars, and none of the three listings named a vet, a lab or a band. We did not average the three, because an average of three numbers for the same bird is still not a price.</p>

          <h5>One Seller's Birds Carried Three Different Prices on a Single Day</h5>
          <p>A classified ad at the bottom of the spread, the seller's own site in the middle, an aggregator at the top, all live at once. The buyer who found the cheap one thought they had found a bargain. They had found the same bird, priced for the audience the platform attracts.</p>

          <h6>Ask Before You Drive: The Three Questions a Fake Listing Cannot Answer</h6>
          <p>What number is on the leg band, which avian veterinarian examined the bird, and can we see it move on a call right now. A real seller answers all three inside a minute. A fake one has a reason, and the reason is usually that the bird is travelling, or that the seller is deployed overseas, or that a courier is standing by. Do not drive to a listing that fails those three. Do not wire money to one either.</p>

          <h3>What a Real Breeder Will Always Let You Do</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-a-real-breeder-always-lets-you-do-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-a-real-breeder-always-lets-you-do-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-a-real-breeder-always-lets-you-do-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Four things an honest breeder always allows before a sale">
          <p>A real breeder lets you check, see, read and dispute before you pay. Run all four on us: the licence is on the federal public search, the video call comes before the {deposit} deposit, the folder opens on that call, and the deposit goes by a method you can dispute. The one sentence you will not hear from a real breeder is any version of “you need to decide today”. A bird that may live sixty years can wait for a phone call.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" decoding="async"></div>

        <!-- ===================== §6 · WHAT ARRIVES ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="papers">
          <h2>What Arrives With Every African Grey We Place?</h2>
          <p class="opener">A bird in its closed leg band and a folder of five documents: the written health guarantee, the avian veterinary certificate, the sexing certificate, the captive-bred treaty paperwork and the hatch record. The folder is the same whether the bird is collected in Texas or lands in Oregon.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "five-documents-with-every-african-grey-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "five-documents-with-every-african-grey-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "five-documents-with-every-african-grey-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The five documents that travel with every African Grey we place">

          <h3>Your Appendix I Certificate</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "your-appendix-one-certificate-seal-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "your-appendix-one-certificate-seal-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "your-appendix-one-certificate-seal-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The embossed Appendix I seal that closes every document folder">
          <p><StatementLabel kind="fact" source="https://cites.org/eng/app/appendices.php" />African Greys have sat on Appendix I of the CITES treaty since January 2017, which restricts international commercial trade in wild-caught birds. The captive-bred certificate in your folder records that your bird was hatched here, in this country, from our own pairs, which is what makes owning it and moving it between states an ordinary thing to do.</p>

          <h4>Does Paperwork Change If the Bird Crosses State Lines?</h4>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "does-paperwork-change-across-state-lines-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "does-paperwork-change-across-state-lines-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "does-paperwork-change-across-state-lines-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Why the same document folder travels unchanged between two states">
          <p>No. The folder is the folder. What changes at a state line is only who asks to see it: the airline wants the veterinary certificate on the crate, and a new avian vet in your city will want the whole set at the first visit. Keep it somewhere you can find it in the 2060s, because the bird will still be with you.</p>

          <h5>Interstate Transport Requires the Certificate to Travel With the Bird</h5>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="observed" since={2014} />Every bird we have flown has left with its veterinary certificate fixed to the crate, because the cargo counter asks for it before the crate is accepted, and the rest of the folder rides in the document pouch. We have never posted paperwork separately and we would not: a bird and its papers should arrive together, so that on day one you can match the band on the leg to the number on the page.</p>

          <h6>From Midland: Which Documents Leave With Which Bird</h6>
          <p>All five, with every bird, single or pair. Jins and Jeni leave with two of everything except the guarantee, which is written for the pair. Nothing is “available on request” after the sale, because the request is the sale.</p>

          <h3>Sex Testing and Vet Records</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "sex-testing-and-vet-records-folder-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "sex-testing-and-vet-records-folder-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "sex-testing-and-vet-records-folder-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Two record tabs filed behind every bird we place">
          <p>Every bird here is DNA-sexed by a laboratory before it is listed, because a cock and a hen look identical for life. The sample is a feather or a drop of blood, and the same kind of sample carries the PCR panel for PBFD, avian polyomavirus and psittacosis; <a href="/african-grey-parrot-health-guarantee/">the guarantee page</a> sets out each screen. The board-certified avian veterinarian's certificate comes from an examination in the days before travel, not from a form filled in at hatch. You get the lab's paper and the vet's paper, with the bird's band written on both.</p>

          <h3>Our USDA AWA Licence</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "our-usda-awa-licence-strip-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "our-usda-awa-licence-strip-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "our-usda-awa-licence-strip-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The federal licence strip a buyer can verify before reserving">
          <p><a href="https://www.aphis.usda.gov/awa/public-search" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The APHIS Animal Care public search ↗</a> lists our federal Animal Welfare Act licence, so look it up before you send a deposit. It is in Mark &amp; Teri Benjamin's names, and it means an inspector can and does walk through the aviary. <a href="/cites-african-grey-documentation/">The documentation page</a> shows what each certificate looks like and how to read it against your own bird. Look first, then reserve.</p>
        </section>

