        <!-- ===================== §7 · IF SOMETHING IS WRONG ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="arrival">
          <h2>What Happens If Something Is Wrong on Arrival</h2>
          <p class="opener">You call us the same day, we arrange an avian veterinary visit near you, and the 72-hour written term does what it says. The term has an address behind it, which is the part a classified listing cannot offer at any distance.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "if-african-grey-arrives-unwell-three-steps-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "if-african-grey-arrives-unwell-three-steps-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "if-african-grey-arrives-unwell-three-steps-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Three steps to take if an African Grey arrives unwell">

          <h3>The 72-Hour Term, and Why the Address Behind It Matters</h3>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "seventy-two-hour-term-and-a-real-address-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "seventy-two-hour-term-and-a-real-address-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "seventy-two-hour-term-and-a-real-address-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Why a guarantee needs a real address standing behind the clock">
          <p>Seventy-two hours from the moment you take possession, in writing, plus twenty-four hours on anything transit-related. A longer promise from a seller with no address is worth exactly the paper it is not printed on. Ours is short because it is meant to be used, and it is usable because there is a licensed aviary at the other end of the phone. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-health-guarantee/">The guarantee page</a> carries the wording in full.</p>

          <h4>Who Do You Call First?</h4>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "who-do-you-call-first-if-something-is-wrong-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "who-do-you-call-first-if-something-is-wrong-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "who-do-you-call-first-if-something-is-wrong-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The order of calls to make if a bird arrives unwell">
          <p>Us, then the vet, in that order, on the day. The first call is not about blame; it is so we can help you find an avian practice that can see the bird inside the window, because a general clinic that has never held a parrot is not the visit the term is written for. If it is late and you are worried, call anyway. Mark or Teri answers.</p>

          <h5>Same-Day Contact Is What Keeps the Term Usable</h5>
          <p>A term with a clock on it is only as good as the moment the clock starts, and it starts when you take the bird, not when you get round to emailing. Buyers who call on arrival day, even to say the bird looks fine, are the ones for whom the guarantee has never needed to become an argument.</p>

          <h6>Distance Note: Finding an Avian Vet in Your State</h6>
          <p><a href="https://www.aav.org/search/custom.asp?id=1803" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Association of Avian Veterinarians' find-a-vet search ↗</a> lists member practices by city. Book one before the bird lands, because our 72-hour term only works if a vet who handles parrots sees the bird inside the window. Bring the folder; the leg band gets matched to the lab paper at that first visit.</p>
        </section>

